== ABI v1 section 9: does waiting in a native frame pin a carrier? ==
java.version=21.0.10
carriers=2  tasks=8  wait=300 ms

If the carrier is pinned the run takes about ceil(N/P)*W = 1200 ms.
If it is not, all 8 wait at once and it takes about 300 ms.

  blocking in the native frame   (ak_call_unary)      1222 ms
  parked on a future, completed  (ak_call_unary_cb)   306 ms

  blocking mode: PINS the carrier
  callback mode: does NOT pin

ABI v1 section 9 predicts pin and no-pin, and that is what this reads.
The consequence is the one the specification draws: a host on virtual
threads cannot use the blocking entry point at scale, so the callback mode
is not a convenience -- it is what makes the ABI usable from the idiom Java
is moving to. The completion queue of section 9 is the same shape again: a
thread parked in a drain is in native state and costs a collection nothing.

Not measured here: any of it under load, with a real transport, or with
more carriers than the two this pins against.

## a second point, so the reading is a curve and not a coincidence
